function manual(topic){showDialog('FIELD MANUAL / TRAFFIC OPERATIONS',`<div class="eyebrow">MUNICIPAL / TRAINING DOCUMENT 001</div><h2>A little timing goes a long way.</h2><p>Traffic engineering is a balancing act. A green light releases one queue while another grows. Your job is to use limited street space and time where they do the most good.</p><h3>01 / Read the district</h3><p>The city starts in Watch view. Click an intersection to open its cabinet beside the map; ×, Escape or V closes it. Show controls opens the operations menu without opening a cabinet. Near me requests a fresh location and loads a 1.8 km district centered there. Location is requested again on every load; if it is blocked or unavailable, the saved district or demo keeps running. District lets you choose an area or resume a saved plan. Traffic drills and their scorecards are in the Test inspector tab. Use WASD to move, Q/E to rotate and Z/X to zoom. Tab cycles nearest intersections; I/J/K/L selects up/left/down/right relative to the camera. Enter opens the selected cabinet; F selects nearest. M opens the current district editor. 1–3 selects 1×/3×/10× time; 4–7 selects quiet/normal/busy/rush traffic. H opens the full key guide. Mouse drag, right-drag and scroll remain available. Select a junction marker or click a traffic signal head to open its field cabinet. The live output monitor shows each incoming approach’s red, yellow and green lamps; amber detector LEDs indicate waiting demand. Amber marks your selection; orange road segments indicate queues. Pause or accelerate the simulation using the playback controls.</p><h3>02 / Give each phase its share</h3><p>Each street group has a through stage and optional protected left-arrow stage. A green arrow releases only left-turning cars; through traffic waits on red. When protection is disabled, left-turners yield to opposing traffic on the circular green. Right turns on red are not modeled. Fixed-time signals repeat the green intervals you choose. Start with more green for the busiest street, but leave enough time for side streets. A longer cycle can clear more cars yet make individual waits longer.</p><div class="lesson"><p><strong>Try it:</strong> at the central junction, increase the main street from 18 to 40 seconds and shorten the quieter phase. Apply the plan, then test it.</p></div><h3 id="actuated">03 / Let detectors ask for green</h3><p>In actuated mode, a phase runs for at least its minimum green. Vehicles detected near the junction reset a passage-gap timer. If an opposing approach is waiting and the timer expires, the phase ends by <strong>gap-out</strong>. Opposing demand starts the maximum-green timer; if vehicles keep arriving, it ends by <strong>max-out</strong> when that timer expires. With no opposing call, it rests in green.</p><h3>04 / Give people time to cross</h3><p>The cabinet’s Request crossing button adds a pedestrian call. During the exclusive pedestrian stage every vehicle signal stays red. WALK admits waiting people. Flashing DON’T WALK closes entry while anyone already in the crosswalk finishes; its countdown covers the widest crossing at 1.1 m/s plus a buffer. Vehicles remain red until the crossing is empty. Actuated controllers skip an unused pedestrian stage; fixed-time programs serve it each cycle.</p><h3>05 / Leave room to clear</h3><p>Vehicle stages use 3 seconds of yellow and at least 2 seconds of all-red between conflicting movements. Pedestrian stages retain their full WALK and flashing clearance intervals. These are simplified teaching defaults. Applying a new plan waits for clearance. A coordination offset aligns the start of the cycle with a district clock, helping a platoon reach the next junction during green.</p><h3>06 / Pavement has a price</h3><p>Road works lets you replace an all-way stop with a signal, or convert a signal to stop control. Added lanes can reduce a queue; a left-turn bay separates turning cars from through traffic. Every change spends part of the $50,000 budget. Widening can simply move a bottleneck to the next junction.</p><h3>07 / Follow the demand</h3><p>Open Vehicles or click a moving car. Every request has an origin, destination and route. Gateways on highways and arterials request more traffic than residential boundaries. Local trips use mapped homes and businesses, with modeled activity by time of day. Cyan marks the selected route and amber its destination frontage. Green lane arrows indicate a permitted movement, amber means yield or clearance, and red means hold. Cars still check downstream room and crossing conflicts before entering. Use Traffic load to stress-test future arrivals; existing queues retain their waiting time.</p><h3>08 / Play a junction drill</h3><p>Select a junction, open the Test tab and press Send traffic wave. Choose 10, 30, 60 or 120 cars and a mix of left, straight or right movements. This repeatable synthetic test pauses the district and runs for up to 3 simulation minutes with normal signal, queue and conflict rules. Clear 80% for silver; clear every car within 2 minutes for gold. The board shows real permission states for each incoming approach and its waiting queue. Open the timing program or use the quick green-time controls, then send the same wave again. Return to live district resumes the traffic you left, with your road and timing edits retained. Practice has no ambient pedestrians; use Request crossing to test a pedestrian call. White vehicle cues mean moving, cyan means turning, amber means waiting; the arrow shape still shows a waiting vehicle’s intended turn.</p><h3>09 / Compare fairly</h3><p>Test your plan runs two independent 4-minute simulations with the same random seed, demand profile, departure requests, and route choices. One uses imported controls and lanes; the other uses your edits. Delay includes unfinished trips and departures waiting outside the district. Aim for 20% less vehicle delay without reducing completed trips or pedestrian crossings.</p><h3>What is real here?</h3><p>OpenStreetMap provides roads and building footprints. Nearby, dated Chicago daily counts calibrate demand where they can be matched. Destinations, hourly patterns, building heights without tags, and movement demand remain modeled. This is an educational game prototype, not a validated road design system.</p><p>Signal concepts: <a href="https://ops.fhwa.dot.gov/publications/fhwahop08024/chapter5.htm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FHWA Traffic Signal Timing Manual, Chapter 5 ↗</a></p><button class="primary" id="manual-done">Return to district ↵</button>`);$('#manual-done').onclick=closeDialog;if(topic)setTimeout(()=>$('#actuated')?.scrollIntoView({block:'center'}),50);}
$('#guide-menu').onclick=()=>manual();$('#manual-bottom').onclick=()=>manual();$$('[data-help]').forEach(b=>b.onclick=()=>manual(b.dataset.help));
async function report(){showDialog('PLAN EVALUATION / MATCHED DEMAND',`<div class="eyebrow">DISTRICT ${esc(areaName.toUpperCase())}</div><h2>Running a controlled comparison.</h2><p>Two 4-minute runs. The same departure requests and routes. Original streets against your current plan.</p><div class="boot-progress" style="background:#d3ddc7"></div>`);await new Promise(r=>setTimeout(r,80));
 try{const result=evaluate(world,plans,{period,demand:density});const a=result.baseline,b=result.candidate,improvement=a.delay?((a.delay-b.delay)/a.delay*100):0,passed=improvement>=20&&b.completed>=a.completed&&b.pedCompleted>=a.pedCompleted;showDialog('PLAN EVALUATION / TEST COMPLETE',`<div class="eyebrow">${esc(areaName.toUpperCase())} / ${esc(period.toUpperCase())} DEMAND / SEED 418 / 240 s</div><h2>${passed?'A better commute starts here.':improvement>0?'The district is moving better.':'Every experiment teaches you something.'}</h2><div class="report-cards"><div class="report-card"><small>ORIGINAL PLAN</small><strong>${a.delay.toFixed(1)}<span> s</span></strong><span>average delay per requested trip</span></div><div class="report-card"><small>YOUR PLAN</small><strong>${b.delay.toFixed(1)}<span> s</span></strong><span>average delay per requested trip</span></div></div><div class="report-verdict">${improvement>=0?'↓':'↑'} ${Math.abs(improvement).toFixed(1)}% ${improvement>=0?'less':'more'} delay. ${passed?'Assignment achieved. Your timing improved delay while preserving completed trips and pedestrian crossings.':'Target: 20% less delay, with at least as many completed vehicle trips and pedestrian crossings. Keep experimenting.'}</div><table class="report-table"><thead><tr><th>MEASURE</th><th>ORIGINAL</th><th>YOUR PLAN</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trips completed</td><td>${a.completed}</td><td>${b.completed}</td></tr><tr><td>Trips requested</td><td>${a.generated}</td><td>${b.generated}</td></tr><tr><td>Vehicles queued at end</td><td>${a.queue}</td><td>${b.queue}</td></tr><tr><td>Pedestrians served</td><td>${a.pedCompleted}</td><td>${b.pedCompleted}</td></tr><tr><td>Mean pedestrian wait (s)</td><td>${a.pedDelay.toFixed(1)}</td><td>${b.pedDelay.toFixed(1)}</td></tr><tr><td>Pedestrians waiting at end</td><td>${a.pedWaiting}</td><td>${b.pedWaiting}</td></tr><tr><td>Mean speed (km/h)</td><td>${a.speed.toFixed(1)}</td><td>${b.speed.toFixed(1)}</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These are simulated outcomes for this demand model. They do not predict measured changes on real streets. Budget remaining: $${budget.toLocaleString()}.</p><button class="primary" id="report-done">Return to the district ↵</button>`);$('#report-done').onclick=closeDialog;}catch(e){showDialog('EVALUATION ERROR',`<h2>Test could not finish.</h2><p>${esc(e.message)}</p>`);console.error(e);}}
$('#run-test').onclick=report;$('#report-menu').onclick=report;
